("Stance 177 - Engine and library version drift silently changes kernels and collective defaults, so a layout conclusion has an expiry date and needs a pinned, hashed environment.",
 "Between releases, attention kernels, fused paths, memory allocators and collective defaults change, and any one of these can move a layout comparison by more than the layouts differ from each other. A conclusion recorded without the exact versions and their hashes cannot be reproduced or safely reused after the next upgrade. Falsifiable hypothesis H177: re-running the identical benchmark under a single minor version bump of the serving engine, with configuration held byte-identical, changes at least one arm's measured latency by more than the run-to-run dispersion of that arm, demonstrating that version is a first-class experimental variable (ESTIMATE; derivation: minor releases routinely alter kernel selection and default flags, and the effect of a kernel change is generally larger than the dispersion of a well-controlled repeated run). Controlled experiment: freeze a container image, record image digest, driver, collective library and engine versions, run both arms, then repeat the whole matrix on the next candidate image and diff the results before adopting it. Rollback gate: an upgrade that changes the layout ordering blocks rollout until the layout decision is re-made under the new image; the previously validated image remains the deployed artifact meanwhile.",
 "No environment pinning or version hashing is required, so the conclusion cannot be reproduced or safely carried across an upgrade."),
("Stance 178 - Cost per served token, not latency alone, is the decision variable once the SLO is met, and the two axes have different utilisation profiles.",
 "Once both layouts satisfy the latency SLO, the remaining question is how many GPUs each needs to serve the offered load. A pipeline layout with idle bubbles wastes a fraction of every stage's time, while a tensor layout spends a fraction of every step in collectives; both are utilisation losses but they scale differently with degree and with concurrency. Choosing on latency alone can lock in the more expensive configuration for no user-visible benefit. Falsifiable hypothesis H178: at the highest concurrency where both layouts still meet the SLO, their achieved tokens per GPU-second differ by more than their p95 latency differs in relative terms, so the cost ranking and the latency ranking are not the same ranking (ESTIMATE; derivation: throughput per GPU integrates utilisation loss over the whole step, whereas tail latency reflects the worst-case path; the two summarise different parts of the same distribution and need not agree). Controlled experiment: for each layout find the maximum concurrency meeting the SLO, then report tokens per GPU-second and GPUs required for the target offered load at that point, using measured achieved throughput rather than a nameplate figure. Rollback gate: if the latency winner costs more GPUs for the same offered load, the decision is escalated with both numbers stated rather than resolved silently by whichever metric the benchmark happened to emphasise.",
 "Cost per served token is never computed, so a latency-optimal layout can be adopted at a materially worse GPU cost with no explicit trade-off."),
("Stance 179 - The deliverable is a decision record with preconditions, monitors and an expiry, because an unconditional layout verdict cannot be audited or safely inherited.",
 "Every claim in this comparison is conditional on model, quantization, context band, hit rate, fabric topology, background load, engine image and traffic shape. A verdict that does not carry those conditions is unfalsifiable in operation, because no future observation can be checked against it. The auditable artifact is a record stating the measured claim, the exact conditions, the monitors that assert those conditions still hold, and the date or event at which the claim must be re-established. Falsifiable hypothesis H179: for a representative sample of past layout decisions, at least one recorded precondition is violated in production within a bounded window after rollout, which would show that preconditions drift and that an unconditional verdict is therefore unsafe by construction (ESTIMATE; derivation: traffic mix, cache hit rate and engine version each change on timescales of weeks in an actively developed service, so the probability that all preconditions hold indefinitely is low). Controlled experiment: instrument each precondition as a live metric with a declared valid range, deploy the chosen layout, and record precondition violations over a fixed observation window alongside SLO attainment. Rollback gate: any precondition violation triggers automatic reversion to the previously validated configuration and re-opens the comparison; the decision record expires on a fixed date even if no violation occurs.",
 "No decision record, precondition monitor or expiry is produced, so the conclusion cannot be audited and will be inherited long after its conditions lapse."),
